<section_header_level_1><loc_77><loc_88><loc_205><loc_96>3. The fundamental lemma.</section_header_level_1>
<text><loc_67><loc_97><loc_419><loc_130>LEMMA 2. Let E be a Lebesgue set of positive measure in [0, 1] which is symmetrical about the point 3. Then there exist k 2, 0  0 such that any y c [a, b] can be expressed as y 1/1 with y, 1 e E (1/k, 1-1/k) for a Lebesgue set of distinct values of 1 of measure at least c.</text>
<text><loc_67><loc_131><loc_419><loc_156>Proo. Choose a k such that G = E N (1/k, 1-1/k) has positive measure. For any continuous function f on [0, 1] one has lim 1 1 f(yx) = f(x) uniformly in x, (as f must be uniformly continuous on [0, 1]), thus</text>
<formula><loc_171><loc_160><loc_315><loc_177>\lim _ { \mathfrak { v } \mathfrak { t } } \int | f ( y x ) - f ( x ) | \mu ( d x ) = 0 \colon</formula>
<text><loc_67><loc_180><loc_419><loc_196>Because the continuous functions are dense in L1, (Zaanen [6], Section 30, Ex. 10) this implies</text>
<formula><loc_167><loc_201><loc_318><loc_218>\lim _ { \mathfrak { v } \mathfrak { t } } \int | \chi _ { o } ( y x ) - \chi _ { o } ( x ) | \mu ( d x ) = 0</formula>
<text><loc_68><loc_221><loc_228><loc_229>(xo being the indicator of G). Hence</text>
