<text><loc_87><loc_76><loc_435><loc_92>h(y) = |h(y/1-y)| = (1-y)-1h(y) + (1-y)h(y/1-y) -h(y) | 1 (1-y)-1{h(y)| + |h(y/1-y)| + |h(y)|</text>
<text><loc_87><loc_98><loc_200><loc_105>This completes the proof.</text>
<text><loc_87><loc_106><loc_435><loc_122>The above is now extended to become: Each measurable solution of (iv) and (vi) is bounded on every compact subset of (0, 1).</text>
<text><loc_86><loc_123><loc_435><loc_165>Poor. Let A be the set of positive X such that h(a) - Xh(a/X) is ultimately bounded as a 0. Plainly (a) 1 A, (b) A/ C, (c) A/ C, so A is a multiplicative group. Now let J be an open interval contained in (0, 1) on which h(.) is bounded; we already know that such an interval exists. If 1-x C J, then since (vi) gives</text>
