<text><loc_85><loc_192><loc_435><loc_233>we see that A. Thus A covers a measurable set of positive measure, and so is the whole of (0, 0) (the latter remark follows on noticing that on taking logarithms we have an additive group, which thus contains its own difference set, hence a non-degenerate interval around zero (Zaanen [6], Section 10, Lemma 0)).</text>
<text><loc_85><loc_234><loc_435><loc_259>From Equation (1) above and from (iv) it now follows that each t = 1-x E (0, 1) lies in an open interval of boundedness of h(.) so that h(.) is bounded on each compact subset of (0, 1).</text>
<unordered_list><list_item><loc_84><loc_263><loc_435><loc_304>5. The uniqueness of h(.) We cannot from the argument above deduce the boundedness of h(.) on the whole of (0, 1); if we could, the desired result would follow from the work of Tveberg [5]. We can, however, adapt one of his arguments to obtain further useful information about h(.) Integration of (vi) (Zaanen [6], Section 36, Theorem 1) gives</list_item>
</unordered_list>
<formula><loc_87><loc_307><loc_429><loc_324>( \mu - \lambda ) \hbar { ( } \alpha ) = \int _ { \mu } ^ { * } h ( \gamma ) \, d \gamma + \alpha ^ { 2 } \int _ { \alpha / 1 - \lambda } ^ { \alpha / 1 - \mu } \gamma ^ { - 3 } h ( \gamma ) \, d \gamma - ( 1 - \alpha ) ^ { 2 } \int _ { \lambda / 1 - \alpha } ^ { \lambda / 1 - \mu } h ( \gamma ) \, d \gamma</formula>
<text><loc_83><loc_328><loc_432><loc_344>for 0</text>
<text><loc_83><loc_345><loc_432><loc_406>We cannot, however, appeal to Fadeev's theorem [1] since that would require continuity on the closed interval [0, 1], but we can adapt the argument at the end of Kendall's paper [2] (see his Equation (12) et seq.) . The argument, which uses (v) and (vi), simplifies drastically because of continuous differentiability; it depends on putting h'(t) = E(t/1-t) to obtain E(u) + E(v) = E(w), the unique continuous solution of which is well known. Note that under (iv), (v) 0 (vi). The following has thus been proved:</text>
<text><loc_93><loc_407><loc_432><loc_414>THEOREM 1. The only measurable solutions to (iv) and (vi) are the multiples of</text>
<page_break>
<page_header><loc_85><loc_42><loc_102><loc_49>418</page_header>
<page_header><loc_238><loc_44><loc_282><loc_49>P. M. LEE</page_header>
<text><loc_85><loc_57><loc_437><loc_74>Shannon's function h(t) = -t lg t - (1 - t) lg (1 - t). The uniqueness of Hk for k: > 2 now follows.</text>
<text><loc_85><loc_74><loc_437><loc_99>This result is best possible in the sense that there are nonmeasurable solutions of (iv) and (vi) other than Shannon's function, for example h(t) = -tf (lg t) - (1 - t)f (lg (1 - t)) where f is a non-measurable linear function.</text>
